PR #72  overall: potential_risk
Spec: SPMP CSR accesses silently return zero/ignore writes instead of trapping when SPMP is disabled, preventing S-mode from reliably detecting that its memory protection is absent
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia.c:388-409  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_rmw_ireg
      This is the single dispatch point for ALL VS-level sireg indirect CSR accesses in KVM. It reads CSR_VSISELECT (line 399) via ncsr_read and uses the raw value to dispatch to iprio or IMSIC emulation. There is zero detection of whether SPMP owns the siselect/sireg indirect-register window. If SPMP is active (e.g., configured by firmware), the siselect value read here could be an SPMP-owned index, causing (a) guest AIA state corruption when the index is misinterpreted, (b) SPMP register corruption when KVM writes through sireg under a false AIA assumption, and (c) silent zero-on-read for SPMP-owned indices, making S-mode unable to detect the degradation. The only guard is kvm_riscv_aia_available() which checks the AIA static key — it has no SPMP awareness.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia.c:111-144  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_load
      Restores guest AIA CSR state by writing csr->vsiselect into CSR_VSISELECT (line 131). When SPMP is active and owns the siselect window, this write corrupts the SPMP indirect-register selector without any detection. Subsequent reads/writes through sireg would then access the wrong extension's register space. The nacl_shmem path (line 121) has the same issue. Only guarded by kvm_riscv_aia_available().
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia.c:146-179  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put
      Saves guest AIA CSR state by reading CSR_VSISELECT (line 169) into csr->vsiselect. If SPMP owns the siselect window, this silently captures an SPMP-owned index value as if it were guest AIA state. On the next kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_load, this SPMP value is written back into VSISELECT, corrupting both the SPMP state and causing KVM to operate on the wrong register space. The nacl path (line 159) has the same issue.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:372-420  imsic_vsfile_local_read
      Uses a save/restore pattern on CSR_VSISELECT (save at line 380, restore at line 419) that assumes exclusive AIA ownership of the siselect window. Between save and restore, it performs multiple sireg reads/writes via imsic_vs_csr_read/swap, imsic_eix_read/swap. If SPMP owns siselect, the saved value (line 380) is an SPMP index, which gets blindly restored at line 419 — potentially resetting SPMP's indirect-register selector and leaking/corrupting SPMP configuration state.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:447-488  imsic_vsfile_local_rw
      Same save/restore-VSISELECT pattern (save at line 452, restore at line 487). Performs reads/writes via imsic_vs_csr_read/write and imsic_eix_read/write inside the bracketed section. The save/restore assumes exclusive AIA ownership of siselect; no SPMP detection is performed before manipulating the indirect-register window.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:520-548  imsic_vsfile_local_clear
      Same save/restore-VSISELECT pattern (save at line 529, restore at lines 546-547). Clears IMSIC registers by writing zero through imsic_vs_csr_write and imsic_eix_write. If SPMP is active, these writes through the sireg window land in SPMP register space instead, silently zeroing SPMP configuration entries — a direct cross-extension corruption.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:550-587  imsic_vsfile_local_update
      Same save/restore-VSISELECT pattern (save at line 567, restore at lines 585-586). Restores IMSIC state from MRIF by writing through imsic_eix_set and imsic_vs_csr_write. The writes to sireg through the indirect window would land in SPMP register space if SPMP owns the window, corrupting SPMP configuration with stale guest IMSIC values.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:92-100  imsic_eix_read
      Macro-expanded switchcase dispatches to imsic_vs_csr_read (line 62-68), which writes CSR_VSISELECT then reads CSR_VSIREG. This is the fundamental indirect-register access pattern used at every IMSIC register site. The pattern is unconditionally AIA-specific with isel values from the IMSIC_EIP0/IMSIC_EIE0 ranges. If SPMP owns the siselect/sireg window, writing an IMSIC index into VSISELECT followed by reading VSIREG could: (a) set SPMP's indirect index to a value in SPMP's own range, then read SPMP state thinking it's AIA state, or (b) if the IMSIC index happens to fall in SPMP's valid range, directly interact with SPMP registers.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:132-140  imsic_eix_swap
      Dispatches to imsic_vs_csr_swap which writes VSISELECT then csr_swap's VSIREG. The atomic swap through sireg is architecturally ambiguous when SPMP shares the window — the read-modify-write could operate on SPMP state instead of IMSIC state.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:171-177  imsic_eix_write
      Dispatches to imsic_vs_csr_write which writes VSISELECT then VSIREG. Unconditional write through the indirect-register window without SPMP ownership check.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:208-214  imsic_eix_set
      Dispatches to imsic_vs_csr_set which writes VSISELECT then csr_set's VSIREG. Unconditional set-bits operation through the indirect-register window without SPMP ownership check.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:348-396  (macro definitions)
      These are pure constant definitions documenting the CSR number assignments: CSR_SISELECT=0x150, CSR_SIREG=0x151 (S-level), and CSR_VSISELECT=0x250, CSR_VSIREG=0x251 (VS-level). These numbers are architecturally defined by the RISC-V ISA and are inherently shared between AIA and SPMP — the collision is at the hardware specification level, not a code defect. No runtime logic here. Compliant: the constants correctly reflect the architectural reality.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_aia.h:52-60  struct kvm_vcpu_aia_csr
      Data structure definition holding the vsiselect field as the in-memory shadow of CSR_VSISELECT. This is a passive data container — it stores whatever value KVM's get/put functions capture. The struct itself is not a vulnerability; the risk lies in how it is populated (kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put reads VSISELECT without SPMP awareness) and consumed (kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_load writes it back).
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_aia.h:147-149  KVM_RISCV_VCPU_AIA_CSR_FUNCS
      This macro registers kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_rmw_ireg as the exclusive handler for ALL CSR_SIREG (0x151) traps (base=CSR_SIREG, count=1). Expanded in vcpu_insn.c:113 into csr_funcs[], this is the trap dispatch that unconditionally wires all sireg indirect CSR accesses into KVM's AIA emulation. When SPMP is active, a guest access to siselect/sireg through the VS-level window (or even direct S-level sireg access in some configurations) gets routed to AIA emulation regardless of whether the access was intended for SPMP. The dispatch table has no mechanism to distinguish AIA vs SPMP ownership of the indirect register window.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/kvm.h:90-98  struct kvm_riscv_aia_csr
      UAPI struct exposes the siselect field to userspace via KVM_GET/SET_ONE_REG. This creates a path where siselect state crosses the kernel/userspace boundary. If SPMP is active and the kernel captures/restores a corrupted siselect value, userspace VMMs (e.g., QEMU) would propagate the corrupted state when migrating VMs or snapshotting — silently spreading SPMP state into guest migration streams. The get/set functions (kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_get_csr/set_csr at aia.c:181-226) guard against SSAIA absence but have zero SPMP awareness.
